 of the Council of the Town of Bentley  August 26, 2025  Date and Place   Minutes of the Regular Meeting of the Council of the Town of Bentley  held Tuesday August 26, 2025, at 6:30pm., in the Bentley Municipal Office  In Attendance   Mayor Greg Rathjen  Deputy Mayor Hansen  Councillor Valiquette  Councillor Eastman  Councillor Grimsdale  CAO, Marc Fortais  Call to Order   Mayor Rathjen called the regular council meeting to order at 6:30pm  Indigenous  Acknowledgement   “We acknowledge that we are meeting on Treaty 6 Territory and  Home of Metis Nation Region 3, on land that is part of a historic agreement involving mutuality and respect. We recognize all the many First Nations, Metis, Inuit, and non-First Nations whose footsteps have marked these lands.”  Read by Mayor Rathjen  Agenda  Motion 139/2025   Moved by Deputy Mayor Eastman “THAT the agenda of the August 26, 2025, regular meeting be accepted.”  Carried  Previous Minutes  Motion 140/2025   Moved by Councillor Grimsdale, “THAT the minutes of the July 8, 2025, Regular Meeting of Council be accepted.”  Carried  Financial  a)   Prepaid Cheque Listing – Cheques No. 20250353 to 20250464  Motion 141/2025   Moved by Councillor Grimsdale, “THAT Cheques No. 20250353 to 20250464 be received for information.”  Carried\n\nRegular Council Meeting Minutes August 26, 2025  b)   Council Remuneration Listing  Motion 142/2025   Moved by Deputy Mayor Hansen, “THAT the council remuneration listings for May, June and July be received for information.”  Carried  New Business  a)   Rimbey Legion Veteran Banners Program – Delegation from Rimbey Legion  Motion 143/2025   Moved by Councillor Grimsdale, “THAT the CAO be directed to support the banner project; AND  THAT administration looks for up to 3 months to find a local liaison that can facilitate and coordinate banner requests through the legion; AND  THAT if a local liaison cannot be found within that period of time, THAT the Town of Bentley administration then take on that role and facilitate the banner coordination, working in collaboration with the legion.”  Carried  b)   RFP QU20252 Award – Residential Garbage Collection  Motion 144/2025   Moved by Councillor Valiquette, “THAT Mayor and council authorize the CAO Marc Fortais, to award the contract for RFP QU20252 – Residential Garbage Collection and Disposal to Empringham Disposal, effective September 1, 2025, with a term ending December 31, 2029, in accordance with the submitted proposal.”  Carried  c)   Land Sale 5514 48A Street (Lot 38, Block 1, Plan 232033)  Motion 145/2025   Moved by Councillor Hansen, “THAT Mayor and Council approve the sale of 5514 48A Street (Lot 38, Block 1, Plan 2320333) located in the Tonw of Bentley within the Sunset Heights Subdivision, to Steven Niles and Martena Strang, in the amount of $63,900 (Including any applicable GST) subject to the terms and conditions as outlined below:  Excepting thereout all mines and minerals  Legal Address:   Lot 38, Block 1, Plan 2320333  Civic Address:   5514 48A Street (Lot 38, Block 1, Plan 2320333)\n\nRegular Council Meeting Minutes August 26, 2025  Purchaser   Steven Niles and Martena Strang  Sale Price   The Sale price is $63,900 including any applicable GST. But does not include any development costs or permits.  Environmental Considerations   The subject property is sold on an “as is – where is” basis.  Fees and Disbursements   The purchaser shall be responsible for all legal and registration fees associated with the transaction.  Vendor Conditions   Subject to approval of this agreement by Town of Bentley Council before 9:00pm on August 29, 2025.  Seller will not provide an RPR.  Purchaser Conditions   Financing condition before 9:00pm pm on September 8, 2025  Subject to buyers’ due diligence for building approved home before 9pm, September 8, 2025 . (It is likely this condition may be extended, depending on when the development permit is submitted to our office.)  Completion Day   Contract completed, the purchase price fully paid and vacant possession given to the buyer at 12 noon on January 7, 2026.”  Carried  d)   Advocacy Conversation – Alberta Municipalities – Minister Meeting  Motion 146/2025   Moved by Councillor Hansen, “THAT the discussion surrounding advocacy be moved to the next council meeting.”  Carried  Correspondence  a)   Public Meeting Notice September 11, 2025 – Open House/Information Session Southeast Area Structure Plan.  b)   Lacombe County Council Highlights July 10, 2025.  c)   Legislative Changes to the Freedom of Information and the Protection of Privacy Act repealed and replaced by the following two acts:\n\nRegular Council Meeting Minutes August 26, 2025  The Protection of Privacy Act  https://www.alberta.ca/protection-of-privacy-act  The Access to Information Act  https://www.alberta.ca/access-to-information-act  Motion 147/2025   Moved Councillor Hansen, “THAT the Correspondance item a) to c) be received as information.”  Carried  Other Business  a)   Arena Slab Project Update – CAO  CAO Marc Fortais provided a general update to discuss some of the challenges faced with the slab replacement which included:  •   Eight feet of frost when the old slab was removed, resulted in additional costs and time to remove as quickly as possible so as to not hold up construction.  •   Damaged curbs around the entire exterior wall footprint, requiring additional rebar repair and concrete repair.  •   Removal of additional concrete perimeter slabs along the entire south side of the arena under players benches, penalty box and timekeeper’s box  •   Contaminated Soil disposal from leaking glycol from damaged cooling lines under the slab.  Administration has managed despite these setbacks, to keep the project on budget. It is looking like final numbers will result in a $30,000 overage in a 1.35-million-dollar project.  In addition, some time has been lost in schedule in dealing with the unforeseen items listed above and it is anticipated the rink will be open by early to mid November, which is 1 to 1.5 months behind schedule from the original project completion date of September 30, 2025.  No Motion Was Made regarding this update.  Break   Motion 148/2025   Moved by Councillor Hansen, “THAT Mayor and  Council takes a 15-minute recess at 7:55pm.”  Carried  Mayor Rathjen called the meeting back in session at 8:14pm\n\nRegular Council Meeting Minutes August 26, 2025  b)   In-Camera Session (Personnel/Labour Matter – CAO Evaluations) in accordance with the Access to Information Act – Division 2 Exceptions to Disclosure.  -   (20) Disclosure Harmful to Personal Privacy  -   (22) Confidential Evaluations  -   (29) Advice from Officials  Motion 149/2025   Moved by Councillor Hansen, “that the in-camera meeting of Mayor and Council be closed to the public at 8:15pm, pursuant to section (20), (22) & (29) of the Access to Information Act – Division 2 Exceptions to Disclosure to discuss CAO Evaluations.”  Carried  Motion 150/2025   Moved by Councillor Valiquette, “THAT the in-camera meeting of Mayor and Council be concluded, and that Mayor and Council reconvene in public at 8:28pm”  Carried  Motion 151/2025   Moved by Councillor Hansen, “THAT Mayor Rathjen and Deputy Mayor Eastman, be directed to contact the Town’s Lawyer, Chapman Riebeek regarding the review and renewal of the CAO Contract.”  Carried  Adjournment  Mayor Rathjen adjourned the meeting at 8:30pm  _________________________   _________________________  Mayor Greg Rathjen   CAO Marc Fortais\n\n\n\nSeptember 9, 2025 ,   Regular Council Meeting   –   New Business   –   Town of Bentley Library Boad Appointments   1  Agenda Date:   September 9, 2025  Agenda Item:   New Business:  Town of Bentley Library Board Appointments  LEGISLATIVE REQUIREMENT/AUTHORITY  •   Libraries Act, Revised Statutes of Alberta 2000, Chapter L-11, Current as of May 16, 2024.  •   Bylaw 123/2008 – Bentley Municipal Library Board Bylaw  •   Bylaw 222/2020 – Bentley Municipal Library Board Bylaw Amendment  SUMMARY AND BACKGROUND  The Bentley Library has been in the community since 1949. Its first space was in the Bentley School with the library collection being supplied by volunteers. The first official Board Members were selected in 1949 as well!  In 1972 a fire destroyed the entire collection, and in 1977 a new library was opened at the Bentley Village Office on main street. The Bentley Municipal Library was formed in 1980, which made the library eligible for an annual provincial grant. Funding also came from the Village of Bentley, Parkland Regional Library, the Royal Purple, Bentley Kinsmen and Bentley Lions.  In 1981. The village office moved with the library accompanying it to their new space on main street, and in 1991 the Village Council and Library Board relocated the library to its current location at 5014 49 th   Ave. The Bentley Municipal Library officially opened June 16, 1991. Over the years, the library has continued to grow, hosting programs, acquiring new equipment and technology and in 2012 the library had a major renovation.  The Bentley Municipal Library is not just about books, there are DVD’s, computers and printing services, cake pans, physical literacy (children’s games, yard games, sports equipment), Wii gaming systems and games, graphic novels. They even run a Mini Libraries program from May to September in our local area campgrounds and summer villages.  Libraries Act Requirements for Appointments  Members  4(1) A municipal library board shall consist of not fewer than 5 and not more than 10 members  appointed by the council of the municipality  (2) A person who is an employee of a municipality library board is not eligible to be a member of  that board\n\nSeptember 9, 2025 ,   Regular Council Meeting   –   New Business   –   Town of Bentley Library Boad Appointments   2  (3) Not more than 2 members of the council of the municipality may be members of the municipal  library board at the same time.  (4) A member of a municipal library board is eligible to be reappointed for only 2 additional  consecutive terms of office, unless at least 2/3 of the whole council of the municipality passes a  resolution stating that the member may be appointed as a member for more than 2 consecutive  terms  (5) Appointments to a municipal library board shall be for a term of up to 3 years.  (6) Notwithstanding subsection (5), the term of office of a member continues until a successor is  appointed or the member is reappointed in accordance with subsection (4)  (7) Any vacancy on a municipal library board that reduces or will reduce the number of members of  the board to a number less than 5 shall be filled by the council of the municipality as soon as  reasonably possible.  Town of Bentley Library Board AGM  The Town of Bentley Library Board AGM was held on Wednesday June 11, 2025, and was attended by members forming a quorum. The board called for nominations to fill the following roles:  1.   Chairperson  2.   Deputy Chairperson  3.   Treasurer  4.   Recording Secretary  5.   2 Directors  In addition, the Town of Bentley assigned a municipal representative from Council at the annual Town of Bentley organizational meeting and Councillor Hansen is currently assigned to this role.  At the AGM the following nominations were made and approved by the Bentley Library Board, and now need to be considered by Town of Bentley Mayor and Council for official appointment:  Board Appointments  Chairperson   Kathy Pike for a term from June 2025 to June 2028  Deputy Chairperson   Robin Lemay for a term from June 2025 to June 2027  Treasurer   Maureen Rue for a term from June 2025 to June 2028  Director   Joan Dikau for a term from June 2025 to June 2026  Director   Carol Hamelin for a term from June 2025 to June 2026  Municipal Representative  Councillor Pam Hansen as appointed through the Town of Bentley Organizational Meeting  Councillor Alan Wilson as appointed through the Lacombe County Organizational Meeting  Other Supporting Appointments  Recording Secretary   Tammy Izquierdo for a non-defined term to support the board  Auditor Appointment   Sandra Smyth  This report has been written to seek council approval to officially appoint the board members to the Bentley Municipal Library Board as recommended by the Bentley Municipal Library Board, following\n\nSeptember 9, 2025 ,   Regular Council Meeting   –   New Business   –   Town of Bentley Library Boad Appointments   3  their AGM and in accordance with the attached minutes   (Attachment #1) . In addition, Mayor and Council should also appoint Councillor Alan Wilson as a representative from Lacombe County.  RECOMMENDATION  THAT Mayor and Council appoint the following persons to the Town of Bentley Library Board for the term specified:  Chairperson   Kathy Pike for a term from June 2025 to June 2028  Deputy Chairperson   Robin Lemay for a term from June 2025 to June 2027  Treasurer   Maureen Rue for a term from June 2025 to June 2028  Director   Joan Dikau for a term from June 2025 to June 2026  Director   Carol Hamelin for a term from June 2025 to June 2026  Town of Bentley Council Representative   Pam Hansen until the October Municipal Election  Lacombe County Council Representative   Alan Wilson until the October Municipal Election  ATTACHMENTS:  1,) June 11, 2025, Town of Bentley Library Board AGM meeting minutes  2.) Bylaw 123/2008 – Bentley Municipal Library Board Bylaw  3.) Bylaw 222/2020 – Bentley Municipal Library Board Bylaw Amendment  _________________________________  Marc Fortais, CAO\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSeptember 9, 2025 -   New Business – Sunset Heights Lot Sale – 4814 55 th   Ave (Lot 3, Block 2, Plan 1521374)  Agenda Date:   September 9, 2025  Agenda Item:   Lot Sales Sunset Heights:  4814 55 th   Ave (Lot 3, Block 2, Plan 1521374)  ADMINISTRATIVE RECOMMENDATION  THAT Mayor and Council approve the sale of 4814 55   th   Ave (Lot 3, Block 2, Plan 1521374) located in the Town of Bentley within the Sunset Heights Subdivision, to Billex Enterprises Ltd., in the amount of $49,900 (plus applicable GST) subject to the terms and conditions as outlined in   (Attachment #1)  SUMMARY & BACKGROUND  The Sunset Heights Subdivision (formerly known as New Beginnings) was established in 2014 through the creation of the Summersault Area Structure Plan (passed through Bylaw 178/2014 and approved August 19, 2014). In August of 2018, a foreclosure order was granted on the property and in 2018 the Town of Bentley acquired the land through the issuance of borrowing bylaw 207/2018. Servicing of the subdivision was initially completed throughout 2019 and 2020 based on the original configuration of the lots. In March of 2020 Mayor and Council approved Bylaw 216/2020 to create general architectural standards to ensure that future development within the subdivision would require to be constructed in a way that contributes to an attractive and aesthetically pleasing appearance along the public street. Beginning in 2021 administration revisited the subdivision and Mayor and Council supported a reconfiguration of the lots to Single Family Dwelling Lots as shown in   (Attachment #2).   All subdivision services were consolidated to support the re-design, and the work was completed in January of 2023. In April of 2023, the name of the subdivision was officially changed from New Beginnings to Sunset Heights based on a public survey that took place to determine a new name that aligned with a new future.  Since April of 2023, the Town of Bentley was actively marketing the properties through realtor Dale Russell of REMAX, social media and through an advertising campaign with Black Press Media. Three lots were sold by Mr. Russell with closing dates of May 2024, July 2024, and August 2024.  Mayor and Council reviewed the activity regarding lot sales since 2023, and decided they wished to hire a new realtor in an attempt to gain new market attention and traction. As a result, Jacquie Fletcher from Royal LePage Sylvan Lake was hired at the end of the 1 st   quarter of 2024, to begin a new marketing campaign for the Lots. There has and still is substantial interest in the subdivision and Jacquie has received many inquiries, some low offers for the lots   (which were not accepted) .  To date the following lots have been sold:  •   Lot 1, Block 2, Plan 152-1374   4810 55 th   Ave   Sold in 2024  •   Lot 6, Block 2, Plan 152-1374   4811 56 th   Ave   Sold in 2024  •   Lot 5, Block 2, Plan 152-1374   4813 56 th   Ave   Sold in 2024  •   Lot 2, Block 2, Plan 1521374   4812 55 th   Ave   Sold in 2025\n\nSeptember 9, 2025 -   New Business – Sunset Heights Lot Sale – 4814 55 th   Ave (Lot 3, Block 2, Plan 1521374)  •   Lot 36, Block 1, Plan 2320333   5510 48A St   Sold in 2025  •   Lot 48, Block 1, Plan 2320333   5601 48A St Close   Sold in 2025  •   Lot 40, Block 1, Plan 2320333   5602 48A St Close   Sold in 2025  •   Lot 41, Block 1, Plan 2320333   5604 48A St   Sold in 2025  •   Lot 42, Block 1, Plan 2320333   5606 48A St Close   Sold in 2025  •   Lot 38, Block 1, Plan 2320333   5514 48A St   Sold pending conditions removal.  •   Lot 3, Block 2, Plan 1521374   4814 55 th   Ave   Under consideration by Council  •   Lot 47, Block 1, Plan 2320333   5603 48A St Close   Under consideration by Council  •   Lor 39, Block 1, Plan 2320333   5516 48A St   Under consideration by Council  To date we have sold 3 lots in 2024 and 6 lots so far in 2025. An additional lot which does not formally close until 2026. As well as the 3 lots currently under consideration by council at this September 9, 2025, regular council meeting. If all of these close that will mean 13 lots have sold in the last couple of years.  It should be noted that 3 houses have been fully built and two more are currently under construction, with more to follow. This will result in additional tax base expansion for the Town of Bentley in 2026 and beyond.  Administration has fully executed a Sale Agreement with Billex Enterprises Ltd, conditional on Council Approval of the sale and in accordance with the Chief Administrative Officer Bylaw.  Council approval is required to finalize the disposal of a fee simple interest in land at market value.  BUDGET AND FINANCIAL INFORMATION  4814 55 th   Ave (Lot 3, Block 2, Plan 1521374)  •   The sales price confirmed for the sale of4814 55 th   Ave (Lot 3, Block 2, Plan 1521374) is $49,900 (plus applicable GST)  RATIONAL FOR RECOMMENDATION  •   Administration and Mayor and Council have worked hard to modify the subdivision to appeal to the market, based on public feedback and the market interest that has been shown for single family dwellings.  •   Significant stakeholder engagement and public council meetings have taken place since 2021 to get to this point, and we continue to have interest in beginning development within the subdivision.  •   The Town has a reasonable offer for this lot.  ATTACHMENTS:  1)   Summary – Land Sale Terms and Conditions (4814 55 th   Ave – Lot 3, Block 2, Plan 1521374)  2)   Subdivision Map – Showing Lot to be sold.  _________________________________  Marc Fortais, CAO\n\nAttachment #1  Meeting Date: September 9, 2025  Excepting thereout all mines and minerals  Purchaser   Billex Enterprises Ltd.  Sale Price   The Sale price is $49,900 not including any applicable GST. Does not include any required permitting costs.  Environmental Considerations   The subject property is sold on an “as is – where is” basis.  Fees and Disbursements   The purchaser shall be responsible for all legal and registration fees associated with the transaction.  Vendor Conditions   Subject to approval of this agreement by Town of Bentley Council before 9:00pm on September 10   th   , 2025.  Seller will not provide an RPR.  Purchaser Conditions   Financing condition before 9:00pm pm on October 2, 2025.  Subject to approval of review of lot grades and utility locations, before 9pm, October 2, 2025.  Completion Day   Contract completed, the purchase price fully