 for the base charge, cost recovery is based on partially a fixed base charge and a per cubic meter charge for all water used (rates are calculated based on revenue needed to be generated based on average utilization of water per household)   (Delays in legal review prevented increases for the first two billing cycles in 2025 and this has been reflected in the 2nd t   quarter projection)  - this rate structure will promote conservation as higher water users will pay based on their higher usage, the previous structure of offering 27m3 for the base charge, resulted in almost no revenue generated as average water usage per 2 months was less than 27m3 so no consumption was billed.  - they have also been compared to other rates in central Alberta (Lacombe, Blackfalds, Rimbey, Sylvan Lake, Bowden, Innisfail, High River, Nanton, Ponoka) where the average bill:  •   based on 1m3 per day would be $417.99 every 2 months (this includes water, sewer, storm sewer and garbage collection)  •   based on .5m3 per day would be $263.23 every 2 months (this includes water, sewer, storm sewer and garbage collection)  -   With the proposed rate structure, the average residential bill based on 1m3 per day water usage will be approximately  •   2025 $186.76 per residential customer per 2 months  •   2026 $193.25 per residential customer per 2 months  •   2027 $194.49 per residential customer per 2 months  •   This is a change from the current average in Bentley of $161.34 (in addition metered rates will drop from $1.16 per cubic metre to $1.01 in 2025 and then go to $1.20 in 2026 and 2027\n\n2025 to 2027 2   nd   Quarter Projection Update  Expenditure     Inflationary increase of 2.5% per annum has been applied where appropriate throughout budget     Salaries and wages have been shown with 2.5% CPI index increases per annum to follow anticipated inflation. It should also be noted that in comparison to other expenditure including capital they represent only 19% of the total expenses in 2025 (if capital is removed and we look at true operating expenditure wages represent 32.10% which is below the average wages for municipal employees of 37.0% Fraser institute study 2020)     There is a line item included in the 2025 to 2027 budget summary for capital each year  -   These amounts are typically funded via Government Grants, Franchise Fees or other Government Transfers on the revenue side of the budget  -   Capital for 2025 is funded 68.01% by grants and 31.99% municipal funds  -   2025 budget is skewed on revenue and expenditure side in comparison to other years due to two major capital projects (1 for arena slab and one for Bentley Recreational Fields)     Other Transfers on the expenditure side reflect contributions to reserves to establish long term reserves for the community. Minimal reserve balances have been established in the past, this new strategy to collect additional funds through utility rate increases and slight tax increases will set Bentley up long term for success and future capital replacement.  -   Town of Bentley is also entering into a contract with Baytex Energy to explore the sale of effluent from the lagoons at $1.00 per cubic meter. This has the potential to result in up to $180,000 per year from the sale of $180,000 cubic metres per annum beginning in 2025 (this has not been included in budget estimates as Baytex has indicated they will not utilize the water this year. Future cashflow can be used to fund major projects like Dick Damron Drive Sewer Main Replacement and Streetscaping (more than a $5,000,000 project). If the effluent is sold in a particular year, the funds will be placed directly in reserves.\n\n2025 Q2 Update - Goals and Accomplishments\n\n2025 Goals and Accomplishments Status Update     Enhance Community Safety and Engagement     Promote Affordable Housing to Foster Population Growth     Action: Host community events and engagement to support social networking and community building  - Work with Lacombe Tou