nal Operations Officer in Alberta before taking on this new role. He believes all Alberta RCMP employees are empowered to lead, collaborate, and contribute at all levels, and knows that they are the strength of the service. Through collaboration and partnership with the communities we serve, Deputy Commissioner Daroux knows together we are supporting safer, stronger, and more connected communities across Alberta.  Deputy Commissioner Daroux has 37 years of policing experience and has also served with the Calgary Police Service. He has served as a Deputy Chief in Charge of the Bureau of Community Policing in Calgary, and as the Director General National Crime Prevention and Indigenous Policing Services for the RCMP, among many other operational and administrative roles. Deputy Commissioner Daroux is focused on continuing to build a modern, progressive police service – one that values innovation, embraces change, and reflects the diverse needs of Alberta.  Thank you for your ongoing support and engagement. As your Chief of Police for your community, please do not hesitate to contact me with any questions or concerns.  Best regards,  Staff Sergeant Jay Peden  Chief of Police  Sylvan Lake Detachment\n\n- Page 1 of 12 -  Alberta RCMP – Provincial Policing Report  Detachment Information  Detachment Name  Sylvan Lake  Detachment Commander  Staff Sergeant Jay Peden  Report Date  August 12, 2025  Fiscal Year  2025-26  Quarter  Q1 (April - June)  Community Priorities  Priority #1:   Crime Reduction - Offender management, Court order compliance  Updates and Comments:  During Q1 the members of the Sylvan Lake Detachment had completed 101 Light House checks. With the regular checks conducted there had been one criminal charge laid for Failing to Comply with a Court Release Order. Once again, the consistency of compliance checks has been a benefit in individuals following their release orders.  Priority #2:   Motor Vehicle Traffic - Impaired driving, speeding, intersection safety  Updates and Comments:  Q1 was a successful quarter for traffic enforcement in the Sylvan Lake Detachment area. 23 Impaired drivers were located and charged either criminally or under provincial sanctions. On May long weekend, check stops were conducted all 3 nights of the weekend, no impaired drivers were located as a result. However, members received very positive comments of motorists/community members encouraging members with their presence.  2 more members of the detachment were trained on laser/radar to add to the detachment’s ability for speed enforcement. 138 violation tickets have been issued this quarter, of which 53 are speeding related. Also, of note 2 cellphone tickets and 4 suspended drivers were located this quarter adding to increased road safety.\n\n- Page 2 of 12 -  Priority #3:   Property - Rural Property Crime- thefts and Break and Enters  Updates and Comments:  Q1 there was a total of 17 calls for service for rural break and enters to either a residence, business, or cottage. Only 2 out of the 17 resulted in charges being laid. 15 were deemed insufficient evidence to proceed and 1 was unfounded. 8 out of 17 were break and enters were to lease sites where cooper wire was stolen. In all lease site occurrences, lack of CCTV was an issue. Oil companies have been reminded to update their CCTV on sites. All files reviewed appear to be scored correctly and have appropriate documentation.  Priority #4:   Community Engagement - Communication, Youth Engagement, Increased  Visibility  Updates and Comments:  Throughout Q1 the detachment members and management have been highly active in community engagement. From numerous visits to local schools, engagements with local municipal governments and Crime Watch events to local schools attending the detachment for tours, members are engaged in the community.  One notable event was a series of presentations to the local high school grade nine and ten classes by a detachment member in responses to an increase in violence amongst the youth.  The detachment, members and communities continue to have a strong relationship which will continue to be maintained.  Priority #5:   Member Wellness  Updates and Comments:  During Q1 the Sylvan Lake detachment holds monthly OH&S meetings where instances of impact on members and employees are reviewed and documented outcomes or changes to the work environment are implemented where required.  During the quarter there has been an increase in reporting events due to files/investigations being conducted where members are involved in higher risk or traumatic events. In the event of physical injury, medical treatment is continued to be monitored to determine the ability to work. These injuries range from injured joints, exposure to bodily fluids or drugs/substances. When occurring, management is notified and proper follow up is taking place.  Additionally, stress and psychological trauma is also being monitored and reported via OH&S documentation.\n\n- Page 3 of 12 -  Where noted or required members are being provided supports for personal wellness. This includes professional resources, a check in, or referrals to Peer-to-peer programs.\n\n- Page 4 of 12 -  Community Consultations  Consultation #1  Date   Meeting Type  April 29, 2025   Meeting with Elected Officials  Topics Discussed  (1): Regular reporting information sharing (2): Crime Reduction Initiatives  Notes/Comments :  Members attended to the Summer Villages Council meeting and presented crime stats and the 2025- 2025 annual Performance Plan  Consultation #2  Date   Meeting Type  April 29, 2025   Meeting with Stakeholders  Topics Discussed  (1): Persons Crimes (2): Youth  Notes/Comments :  Members attended to the High School to discuss a response to increased Youth violence in the school itself and what the Detachment members are encountering outside of the school hours. a healthy conversation took place in which reporting pathways, police attendance both informal and informal to the sch